Exercise: Coherent forecasting

Visually compare base and coherent forecasts.



Code
fit_pbs |&gt; 
  forecast(h = "2 years") |&gt; 
  filter(if_all(c(ATC1, ATC2, Concession), is_aggregated)) |&gt;
  autoplot(
    pbs_atc |&gt; filter(Month &gt; yearmonth("2000 Jan")),
    alpha = 0.4
  )

Decomposing multiplicative data
Total Australian retail turnover is multiplicative, so we cannot use STL directly.

aus_retail |&gt;
  summarise(Turnover = sum(Turnover)) |&gt; 
  autoplot(Turnover)

Decomposing multiplicative data
Log transform the data to make multiplicative patterns additive.

aus_retail |&gt;
  summarise(Turnover = sum(Turnover)) |&gt; 
  autoplot(log(Turnover))

Decomposing multiplicative data
Box-Cox transformations are more flexible.
\(\lambda = 0\) is \(f(x) = \log(x)\), \(\lambda = 1\) is \(f(x) = x\).

aus_retail |&gt;
  summarise(Turnover = sum(Turnover)) |&gt; 
  autoplot(box_cox(Turnover, lambda = 0.2))

STL Decomposition
autoplot() plots the components.

aus_retail |&gt;
  summarise(Turnover = sum(Turnover)) |&gt; 
  model(STL(box_cox(Turnover, lambda = 0.2))) |&gt; 
  components() |&gt; 
  autoplot()

STL Decomposition
window sizes adjust the fit.

aus_retail |&gt;
  summarise(Turnover = sum(Turnover)) |&gt; 
  model(STL(box_cox(Turnover, lambda = 0.2) ~ season(window = Inf))) |&gt; 
  components() |&gt; 
  autoplot()

